= The Schlageter Speech

On 20 June, at a session of the Comintern's Executive, Karl Radek gave a speech on Albert Leo Schlageter, a Freikorps veteran shot by the French for sabotage in the Ruhr. Radek called him a "wanderer into the void" and invited German nationalists to find their way to the workers' side against the Entente.

The "Schlageter course" now runs through the party press. Communist speakers debate with nationalist students and völkisch groups. Many members are confused.

- @amplify: Take the course seriously: debate the nationalists and win their youth.
- @tolerate: Repeat the line in the paper but keep it away from the factories.
- @oppose: Object to the course, inside the party.

@amplify
on-arrival: {!
Q.members += 8;
Q.spd_relation -= 6;
Q.right_moscow += 3;
Q[Q.player + '_moscow'] += 4;
!}

Communist and nationalist speakers share platforms. A few young nationalists join the party. The SPD press is delighted to quote the Rote Fahne next to the völkisch papers.

@tolerate
on-arrival: {!
Q.members += 3;
Q.spd_relation -= 2;
!}

The party prints the speeches and goes on with its factory work. The episode passes without much effect either way.

@oppose
on-arrival: {!
Q[Q.player + '_moscow'] -= 6;
Q.spd_relation += 3;
Q[Q.player + '_dissent'] += 4;
!}

You argue that a workers' party cannot borrow the nationalists' heroes. Radek takes it personally.
